#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1380.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167732} :activity/id #uuid "d2daf8ec-962f-5cd4-9714-2cfebccb518d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048878 :topic/id #uuid "c8e7edcb-eb86-52ac-9a3b-92e4b445b353" :topic/math-academy-id 3260} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167626 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166791 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166629 :question/id #uuid "b6135ab8-d329-5646-aeb7-dea8b38e7606" :question/math-academy-id "e-8420"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f04980f8-35db-5cf4-8a4d-8e9f0f23fb1e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049599}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167138} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167400} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536872} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536879} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536885} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549034}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Hyperbolic Functions"} :step/id #uuid "96a09359-527a-5e5b-b3ff-0751c279f524" :step/math-academy-id 631340 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167627}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167627 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166792 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166630 :question/id #uuid "555cf939-5320-5230-bcef-4828210fbc34" :question/math-academy-id "e-8421"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e14a3673-7b90-5f81-b73a-c2894f07c561" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536814} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536864} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536894} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536900} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549013}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Quotient of Hyperbolic Functions"} :step/id #uuid "2b9ea8f0-27f6-52e0-95a8-a6c153a1617f" :step/math-academy-id 631343 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167628}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167628 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166793 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166631 :question/id #uuid "27ef207b-1337-5a3b-92cc-0d7a8b6bb13e" :question/math-academy-id "e-8422"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7fafc0a-8fff-5c9d-a55a-f25ce3c1d729" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050489}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167403} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167404} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536826} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548976} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549045}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Hyperbolic Integral Using a Substitution"} :step/id #uuid "7dbc74d1-d41f-5d76-806c-c0b277105b07" :step/math-academy-id 631346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167732 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168117 :tutorial/content "It's possible to convert every trigonometric identity to a corresponding *hyperbolic* identity using **Osborn's rule.** \n\nTo apply Osborn's rule, we follow two steps:\n\n1\\. Replace every trigonometric function with its corresponding hyperbolic function.\n\n2\\. For any product of *two trigonometric sines*, change the sign of the corresponding product of hyperbolic sines.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the Pythagorean trigonometric identity:\n\n$$\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\cos^2 x \\:{\\color{blue}{\\boldsymbol +}}\\: \\sin^2 x = 1\n$$\n\nNotice that this trigonometric identity contains a product of two sines. Therefore, to find the corresponding hyperbolic identity, we change each trigonometric function to its corresponding hyperbolic counterpart and change the sign in front of the corresponding product of sines. \n\nThus, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is\n\n$$\n\\cosh^2 x \\:{\\color{red}{\\boldsymbol -}}\\: \\sinh^2 x = 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe can check that this identity is indeed true using the definitions of the hyperbolic sine and cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cosh}^{2}⁡x-{sinh}^{2}⁡x & ={(\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}))}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}))}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}{({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})}^{2}-{({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({e}^{2x}+2+{e}^{-2x}-({e}^{2x}-2+{e}^{-2x})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\cancel{{e}^{2x}}+2+\\cancel{{e}^{-2x}}-(\\cancel{{e}^{2x}}-2+\\cancel{{e}^{-2x}})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b6c4b399-c2b4-5fcb-a5b8-60bb6d23c29a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5552 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4693d31f-c75b-551c-85f0-e6ea3040b97e" :step/math-academy-id 631334 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167804}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167761 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166872 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166725 :question/id #uuid "e05932ad-3ee7-5b8a-a0c9-8e8ac50990d0" :question/math-academy-id "e-9881"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c7ddaa7-f225-56f3-966a-f214152b1ad0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048192} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048867} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050125}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167464} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167465} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536817} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536878}] :knowledge-point/title "Deriving a Hyperbolic Identity Using Osborn's Rule"} :step/id #uuid "2f36f0be-0a1c-5d94-9a98-49a786e635b5" :step/math-academy-id 631336 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167803}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167803 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168152 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to calculate the integral\n\n$$\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sinh^2 x}\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we note the following hyperbolic identity:\n\n$$\n\\csch{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sinh{x}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sinh^2{x}} \\,\\textrm{d}x\n= \\int \\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\sinh{x}}\\right)^2 \\,\\textrm{d}x\n= \\int \\csch^2{x} \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nFrom here, we can see that this is just a standard hyperbolic integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sinh^2{x}}\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\int \\csch^2{x}\\,\\textrm{d}x = -\\coth{x} + C\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f612c2c5-2457-59c4-899a-0847e3f278a5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7913 :tutorial/title "Integration Using Hyperbolic Identities"} :step/id #uuid "02f143e3-76ee-5144-89d8-7142626764d4" :step/math-academy-id 631339 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167626}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167804 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168153 :tutorial/content "When applying Osborn's rule, we must be aware that sometimes a product of two sines is \"hidden.\"\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the following trigonometric identity:\n\n$$\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n1 \\:{\\color{blue}{\\boldsymbol +}}\\: \\tan^2 x = \\sec^2 x\n$$\n\nAt first glance, it may seem that there is no product of sines, and we do not need to change any signs in the corresponding hyperbolic identity.\n\nHowever, recall that\n\n$$\n\\tan x = \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}\n$$\n\nwhich implies that\n\n$$\n\\tan^2 x = \\dfrac{\\sin^2 x}{\\cos^2 x}.\n$$\n\nSo, there is a hidden product of sines within the $\\tan^2 x$ term, and we need to flip the sign in front of this term when writing down the corresponding hyperbolic identity.\n\nThus, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is\n\n$$\n1 \\:{\\color{red}{\\boldsymbol -}}\\: \\tanh^2 x = \\sech^2 x.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0cd7ebe1-9de5-5914-a1e9-4b88c94e5a0e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7914 :tutorial/title "Osborn’s Rule in Cases Where a Product of Sines Is Hidden"} :step/id #uuid "25c4fda7-21cf-54ba-8f5a-d6ff1375cd9b" :step/math-academy-id 631335 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167761}}] :activity/title "Integration Using Basic Hyperbolic Identities" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186167026}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046645} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549095} :activity/id #uuid "edf24058-88a8-5581-b794-478468ae810a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048878 :topic/id #uuid "c8e7edcb-eb86-52ac-9a3b-92e4b445b353" :topic/math-academy-id 3260} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549095 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549034 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ed2b512-fd94-527c-8aa7-14eb2e253d5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csch}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548448} {:answer/id #uuid "677c0d5e-9a4f-53b6-831c-38e8d4e4c8aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sech}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548550} {:answer/id #uuid "d42ea941-f8f4-516d-9716-11327749134c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548769} {:answer/id #uuid "ef82bee9-2b28-57a1-b630-a8d70ae2e61d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548829} {:answer/id #uuid "efa87d3d-378e-520b-80c5-c18393473402" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x+\\operatorname{csch}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548830}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548829} :answer-field/id #uuid "d537a454-077e-5bec-8d5e-c804712b982d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548961}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "97f38e8d-e7da-59bd-ba74-56efb7ebb5d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-95741" :question/problem "$$\n\n$\\int \\operatorname{coth}⁡x(2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x+\\operatorname{csch}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometry identity \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{coth}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply out the parentheses and then use the identity above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{coth}⁡x(2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x+\\operatorname{csch}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{csch}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\operatorname{csch}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\operatorname{csch}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4a24ff8d-4408-5086-a362-5fda8f4d6863" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549107}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549100 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549045 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4094b1d6-e49d-5a4c-8130-b945454b28db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{49}{65}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548478}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548478} :answer-field/id #uuid "25b5f170-7d01-575f-9c9a-1bf9fe7b0568" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548883}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ba7d332a-6786-594a-94f9-7b21daa8d15e" :question/math-academy-id "q-352941" :question/problem "$$\n\nEvaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}3\\operatorname{sech}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\nFirst, we find the hyperbolic identities that correspond to the following trigonometric identities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identities are\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{sech}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}3\\operatorname{sech}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\operatorname{sech}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3x)}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3x)}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3x)}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=3x.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=3\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=3x.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡8$ |\n\nNow, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\operatorname{sech}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8}\\operatorname{sech}⁡(u)\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(u)\\,\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sech}⁡(u){|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡8} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sech}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡8)+\\operatorname{sech}⁡(0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡8)}+1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(\\frac{8+{8}^{-1}}{2})}+1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(\\frac{65}{16})}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{49}{65}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5732f6ba-37ef-5624-8851-8bc3a6396c11"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549107 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548976 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d5ae3f1-9426-5b71-9c8a-39ff341b203d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{240}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548469} {:answer/id #uuid "adaf5e80-f765-530e-bb4c-2a9cb2a2639f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{75}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548698} {:answer/id #uuid "e3b3d1f0-bf8f-570f-a904-dec2d7f66618" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{45}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548799} {:answer/id #uuid "efbf3afe-ee5e-5490-a69f-dee8cd1d6186" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{120}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548831} {:answer/id #uuid "f4838b2c-2ff8-52d4-bfdd-390ae6cc1518" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{23}{110}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548841}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548469} :answer-field/id #uuid "fca0c653-f489-5a0b-b300-4fbf7f846779" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548974}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0266df41-acf0-5289-8790-ab60c8f211ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-96504" :question/problem "$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\nFirst, recall the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csch}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{csch}^{2}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=2x.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u=\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=2x.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡4$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡9$ |\n\nNow, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{csch}^{2}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}{csch}^{2}⁡u\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}{csch}^{2}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{coth}⁡u){|}_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡9} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)+\\operatorname{coth}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}+1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}-1}+\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+1}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{{9}^{2}+1}{{9}^{2}-1}+\\frac{{4}^{2}+1}{{4}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{41}{40}+\\frac{17}{15}) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{240}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7045c248-0444-5e93-afb7-d434ed35d42a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549123}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549121 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549013 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a0d7365-f518-5311-a5f3-c5f826b81b2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{e}^{4}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548467}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548467} :answer-field/id #uuid "40c090a2-44ae-5ef6-9984-cc694c4d8efa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548897}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "735acc88-33c3-52d8-953c-ec2e9e0254cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-346480" :question/problem "$$\n\nEvaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{{coth}^{2}⁡x}{2{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{{coth}^{2}⁡x}{2{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {coth}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow we need to find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometric identity \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{coth}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the above identity to write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {coth}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{csch}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{coth}⁡2-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{coth}⁡(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{coth}⁡(1)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{coth}⁡2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{{e}^{2}+1}{{e}^{2}-1}-\\frac{{e}^{4}+1}{{e}^{4}-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{({e}^{2}+1)^{2}-({e}^{4}+1)}{({e}^{4}-1)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{e}^{4}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "867e285f-fea0-51f6-8984-0117bc47c407" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549100}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549123 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167138 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9751268b-065e-530e-8f61-524e89ea5d81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x-2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548649} {:answer/id #uuid "c58bd0f4-783c-5d87-a549-3f629b85dc92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csch}⁡x-2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548746} {:answer/id #uuid "d5845fec-c7b4-59ef-afad-7f9f315af72e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x-2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548771} {:answer/id #uuid "efcfb69a-cb84-52fa-83e3-8c5deda2d628" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x-2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548832} {:answer/id #uuid "f0f06524-c375-54b5-86ff-27ccebc4476b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{sinh}^{2}⁡x-2x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548835}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548832} :answer-field/id #uuid "20dec17a-ebe7-549a-9b29-43f35de681f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163906}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "941a2692-f4e7-54c6-8e94-3c936c9ba118" :question/math-academy-id "q-165866" :question/problem "$\\int \\operatorname{sech}⁡x(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "$$\n\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sech}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply out the parentheses and then use the identity above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sech}⁡x(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x-2)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x-\\int 2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x-2x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8ae138ac-bc59-5ae3-a741-0a8c4c2c3c10" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549121}}] :activity/title "Integration Using Basic Hyperbolic Identities" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548356}]}
